# Overview
Deploys a small Go service that mints short-lived kubeadm bootstrap tokens
and serves a per-node `curl | bash` join script -- so adding a physical or
external machine (no BMC, no CAPI infrastructure provider available) to the
cluster is as simple as declaring it in `nodes:` below and running one
command on the target box.

Every request to `GET /bootstrap/{name}` requires `Authorization: Bearer
<fetchToken>` and a `name` that's actually declared in `nodes:` -- anything
else gets 401/404. The RBAC this chart creates is scoped to
`create`/`get`/`delete` on Secrets in `kube-system` only (the same
permission `kubeadm token create` itself needs), nothing broader.

Only `osFamily: ubuntu` is implemented today.

## Adding this helm repository

To add the helm repository, run the following commands:

```bash
helm repo add janip81 https://janip81.github.io/helm-charts/
helm search repo node-bootstrap
```

`values.yaml` files for the charts can be found in the `charts/[chartname]` directories.

## TL;DR

```bash
helm repo add janip81 https://janip81.github.io/helm-charts/
helm install my-app-name janip81/node-bootstrap -f YOUR-OWN-VALUES.yaml
```

[Helm](https://helm.sh) must be installed to use the charts.  Please refer to
Helm's [documentation](https://helm.sh/docs) to get started.

If you had already added this repo earlier, run `helm repo update` to retrieve
the latest versions of the packages.  You can then run `helm search repo
<alias>` to see the charts.

To install the <chart-name> chart:

    helm install my-<chart-name> <alias>/<chart-name>

To uninstall the chart:

    helm delete my-<chart-name>

## Prerequisites

- [Kubernetes](https://kubernetes.io/)
- [Helm 3.1.0](https://helm.sh)

